    "q-346722": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346722",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n9-2x=A(x-3)^{2}+Bx(x-3)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(0) & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0)(0-3)+C(0) \\\\ 9 & =9A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(3) & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3)(3-3)+C(3) \\\\ 3 & =3C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2(1) & =A(1-3)^{2}+B(1)(1-3)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =4A-2B+C \\\\ 7 & =4(1)-2B+1 \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{9-2x}{x(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
